379:
(a) In agreement with the governing officials, there is established the city emergency management office. The mayor, with the concurrence of other city officials, shall appoint a deputy coordinator of emergency management for the city.
(b) When appointed, the deputy coordinator shall be on the staff of the county emergency management director/coordinator and is charged with the following duties:
(1) To represent the governing officials of the city on all matters pertaining to civil preparedness;
(2) To assist city officials in organizing city departments for emergency operations;
(3) To develop, in conjunction with city departments and agencies, an emergency preparedness plan for emergency functions set forth in section 10-19. The preparedness plans will be in support and in consonance with the county emergency operations and disaster plan and shall be submitted to the governing officials of the city and thence to the county emergency management director/coordinator for approval;
(4) To render the reports as planning, activities and the like as required by governing officials and the county director/coordinator in keeping with good programming;
(5) To coordinate the activities of the city department heads during a declared emergency and in support of the county emergency management operations and disaster plan.
(Code 1985, § 3-3-2; Code 2004, § 37.02; Ord. No. 231, 8-12-1974)

381:
In the event of manmade or natural disaster, enemy attack upon the United States or any other emergency which may affect the lives and property of the citizens of the city, the mayor and city council may declare that a state of emergency exists and thereafter shall have and may exercise for the period as the state of emergency exists or continues, the following emergency powers:
(1) To enforce all rules, laws, and regulations relating to emergency management and to assume direct operational control over all city emergency management resources;
(2) To seize, take for temporary use, or condemn any property for the protection of the public;
(3) To sell, lend, give, or distribute any property or supplies among the inhabitants of the city, and to maintain a strict accounting of property or supplies distributed and for funds received for the property or supplies;
(4) To perform and exercise the other functions and duties and take the emergency actions as may be necessary to promote and secure the safety, protection and well-being of the inhabitants of the city.
(Code 1985, § 3-3-4; Code 2004, § 37.04; Ord. No. 231, 8-12-1974)

391:
(a) No construction may be initiated within the city for any residential building of any type which:
(1) Employs a gravity tank type, flushometer-valve, or flushometer-tank that uses more than an average of 1.6 gallons of water per flush;
(2) Employs a showerhead that allows a flow of more than an average of 2.5 gallons of water per minute at 60 pounds per square inch of pressure;
(3) Employs a urinal that uses more than an average of 1.0 gallons of water per flush;
(4) Employs a lavatory faucet or lavatory replacement aerator that allows a flow of more than 2.0 gallons of water per minute; or
(5) Employs a kitchen faucet or kitchen replacement aerator that allows a flow of more than 2.5 gallons of water per minute.
(b) No construction of any commercial building initiated within the city for any commercial building of any type which does not meet the requirements of subsection (a) of this section.
(c) The requirements of subsection (a) of this section shall apply to any residential construction and to any commercial construction which involves the repair or renovation of or addition to any existing building when the repair or renovation of or addition to the existing building includes replacement of toilets or showers or both.
(Code 2004, § 53.03; Ord. No. 316, 5-13-1991)

392:
New construction and the repair or renovation of an existing building shall be exempt from the requirements of section 12-20 when:
(1) The repair or renovation of the existing building does not include the replacement of the plumbing or sewage system servicing toilets, faucets or showerheads within the existing buildings;
(2) When the plumbing or sewage system within the existing building, because of its capacity, design, or installation would not function properly if the toilets, faucets or showerheads required by this article were installed;
(3) The system is a well or gravity flow from a spring and is owned privately by an individual for use in the individual's personal residence; or
(4) Units to be installed are:
a. Specifically designed for use by the handicapped;
b. Specifically designed to withstand unusual abuse or installation in a penal institution; or
c. Toilets for juveniles;
(5) The owner, or his agent, of a building undergoing new construction or repair or renovation who is entitled to an exemption as specified in this section shall obtain the exemption by applying at the office of the building inspector for the city. A fee, as established by the mayor and city council and provided in the city fee schedule, shall be charged for the inspection and issuance of the exemption.
(Code 2004, § 53.04; Ord. No. 316, 5-13-1991)

