419:
It shall be unlawful, and is hereby declared a nuisance, for any person:
(1) To deposit, collect, store, maintain, scatter upon, or permit to be deposited, collected, stored or to remain upon private property any garbage, junk, trash, refuse, abandoned property, or abandoned vehicle, all as defined herein, which is unsightly, unhealthy or hazardous.
(2) To permit a condition or use of a premises or of a building exterior which is detrimental to the property or health of others or which causes or tends to cause more than slight diminution in the value of other property in the neighborhood in which said premises is located will be considered unsightly, unhealthy, and/or hazardous. This includes, but is not limited to, the depositing on, scattering over, or keeping upon the premises any of the following:
a. Unsheltered storage of lumber, pipe, junk, windows, wire, tires, refuse, garbage, trash, unstacked brick or block, or major component parts of a vehicle;
b. Storage of abandoned, discarded or unused appliances, machinery, equipment, furniture, motor vehicles, cans, or containers;
c. Any compost pile which is of such nature as to spread or harbor disease, emit unpleasant odors or harmful gases, or attract rodents, or other obnoxious animals or insects, provided that the presence of earthworms in a compost pile shall not constitute a nuisance;
d. Unsanitary items, deleterious septic or disease producing materials, gases, or fluids, unless such material is contained in containers or vessels which prevent leakage and escape, and which deny access to humans, flies, insects, rodents, and animals, and such material is kept in compliance with all federal, state, and local laws and regulations concerning it;
e. Toxic or poisonous materials, gases, or fluids, unless such material is contained in containers or vessels which prevent leakage and escape, and which deny access to humans, flies, insects, rodents and animals, and such material is kept in compliance with all federal, state, and local laws and regulations concerning it;
f. Piles of dirt, gravel, sand, brick, concrete block, roofing, metal, or other similar items, substances, or by-products.

